 get at speaking  it  however uh within one year of arriving  in japan i was conducting business in  japanese giving talks in japanese i was  working at the canadian embassy  um  and uh so i learned it rather quickly i  did it in on my own i didn't go to  school  i didn't have a tutor  i just listened and and read a lot and i  watched tv i used the first i could  understand the baseball games on tv and  eventually i moved from there to  political discussions because again  the newspapers were a little easier to  read because there's more characters  there and what was most difficult of  course was movies and soap operas and  those kinds of things that were on tv  but certainly over the course of the  nine years my japanese improved and i  think this is another important point  you can very quickly arrive at a level  in a language where you can actually  communicate and do stuff  but then to get better and better and  better and better it all takes a long  time  so uh yeah nine years  what did i do okay i read  i i have to admit that i used a lot of  you know i read what i read books that  had  vocabulary lists  rarely did i use  the dictionary because it's very  frustrating it's hard to look things up  and you forget them right away so i used  material that had  a vocabulary list for each lesson  whatever i could find um i went through  all the sort of grammar textbooks if you  want you know lesson one uh you know my  name is mr suzuki lesson two this is my  family lesson three uh at the grocery  store and so  i went through as quickly as possible i  never do exercises as i've said before  and i don't remember any of the  grammatical terms  uh  so  you know i decided to google here  japanese grammar learn japanese and the  resources available today are phenomenal  and if any of you want to start learning  japanese or if you are in the midst of  learning japanese you should certainly  make yourself aware of all the resources  that are out there  there's one  you know i googled japanese grammar the  first thing i got was of course a thing  from wikipedia which is hopeless the  japanese language has a regular  agglutinative verb morphology with both  productive and fixed elements in  language typology it has many features  divergent from most european languages  its phrases are exclusively head final  and compound sentences are exclusively  left branching blah blah  you know if that was my only source i  would quit i wouldn't go beyond that to  me that is hopeless  uh you don't need it to be that  complicated  there's a thing called the  learningpage.com there's kim's guide to  japanese and many more resources find  the one that you like  now  with a lot of these resources especially  take him and the  japanese page i think excellent in that  they provide  uh sort of sample sentences of the  different sort of patterns in japanese  and the japanese page actually has audio  it's actually an excellent resource they  both are i think take him goes into too  much detail  about what can happen and could happen  and stuff you don't really need that but  they're both good and they both have  iphone apps as well  uh i think kay can take kim's is free  and the japanese page is like eight  euros or something but it looks to be  well worth it if i were learning  japanese i would get that app  now how to use these resources you can't  learn a language by just reading all  these isolated phrases so this has to be  done in conjunction with again  a lot of listening and reading  and so you listen and read then you go  back to these resources and a lot at  first it seems overwhelming there are so  many patterns so many things that are  difficult how can i ever learn this but  as  you become familiar with some of these  patterns you start to notice other  patterns so you go back to the you know  you whatever you decide to use as your  resource for how the language works the  different patterns of the language then  you go back to listening and rea